# G025 — Guestbook / Journal

*v2. See `guestbook.md` for prose: first immutable data structure,
append-only log, chronological ordering as temporal sequence,
author attribution as provenance, audit trail as the resulting
compound property.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Daily Note as Journal

[daily-journal @date
    IN ORDER TO
        let every agent append their contribution to today's journal
        keep the log append-only so "who wrote what when" always answers
        and produce a queryable audit trail as a side-effect

    @journal <- [journal[for-date {date: @date}]]

    sync @events {
        append @agent @content ->
            [journal[add-entry
                      {journal: @journal,
                       author: @agent,
                       content: @content}]]
            ;; timestamp auto-assigned; no edit, no delete

        query-since @t ->
            -> [journal[get-entries
                          {journal: @journal, since: @t}]]

        query-by-author @a ->
            -> [journal[get-entries
                          {journal: @journal}]]
               where author is @a
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[journal
    new
    for-date         {date}
    add-entry        {journal, author, content}     ;; ONLY mutation
    get-entries      {journal, since?}               ;; chronological
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: append-only `where` mutation is exactly one verb

[journal[add-entry {journal: @j, author: @a, content: @c}]
    IN ORDER TO
        record an immutable entry without any edit or delete path
        because the log IS the truth and history cannot be rewritten

    @entry <- {author: @a, content: @c, ts: now}
    @j:entries <- @j:entries + @entry
    return (@j, @entry)
]

Same discipline G094's log file, G100's commit chain, and Git
itself use. No edit verb exists. No delete verb exists. Past
entries are permanent — rewriting is impossible by construction.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @j <- [journal[new]]
    [journal[add-entry {journal: @j, author: "nathan",
                         content: "Rosetta Stone second pass begun"}]]
    [journal[add-entry {journal: @j, author: "tasks",
                         content: "42 .dpn files written yesterday"}]]
    [journal[add-entry {journal: @j, author: "tasks",
                         content: "databases and files closed today"}]]

    -> [journal[get-entries {journal: @j}]]
    ;; -> three entries, chronological order preserved

    -> [journal[get-entries {journal: @j}]] where author is "tasks"
    ;; -> two entries, filtered by author
]
